{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011n6eyWSUt2XKXKLjemuiyg","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Scoring Scales\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\n- 5 = \"Positive that my evaluation is correct\"\n- 4 = \"Quite sure...unlikely...I missed something\"\n- 3 = \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- 2 = \"Willing to defend...fairly likely I missed details\"\n- 1 = \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n## Soundness\n- 5 = \"Excellent: one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- 4 = \"Strong: sufficient support for all claims\"\n- 3 = \"Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\"\n- 2 = \"Poor: main claims not sufficiently supported\"\n- 1 = \"Major Issues: not sufficiently thorough\"\n\n## Excitement\n- 5 = \"Highly Exciting: would recommend to others\"\n- 4 = \"Exciting: would mention to others\"\n- 3 = \"Interesting: might mention points to others\"\n- 2 = \"Potentially Interesting: doesn't resonate with me\"\n- 1 = \"Not Exciting: doesn't resonate with me\"\n\n## Overall Assessment\n- 5 = \"Consider for Award\" (top 2.5%)\n- 4.5 = \"Borderline Award\"\n- 4 = \"Conference: could be accepted\"\n- 3.5 = \"Borderline Conference\"\n- 3 = \"Findings: could be accepted to Findings\"\n- 2.5 = \"Borderline Findings\"\n- 2 = \"Resubmit next cycle\"\n- 1.5 = \"Resubmit after next cycle\"\n- 1 = \"Do not resubmit\"\n\n## Key Differentiation Guidance\nMain conference papers require stronger novelty and impact; Findings emphasize \"soundness and reproducibility.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"e3beeba9-e118-4ae4-8b14-aa081f674ad8","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:12:14.742Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Scoring Scales\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\n- 5 = \"Positive that my evaluation is correct\"\n- 4 = \"Quite sure...unlikely...I missed something\"\n- 3 = \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- 2 = \"Willing to defend...fairly likely I missed details\"\n- 1 = \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n## Soundness\n- 5 = \"Excellent: one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- 4 = \"Strong: sufficient support for all claims\"\n- 3 = \"Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\"\n- 2 = \"Poor: main claims not sufficiently supported\"\n- 1 = \"Major Issues: not sufficiently thorough\"\n\n## Excitement\n- 5 = \"Highly Exciting: would recommend to others\"\n- 4 = \"Exciting: would mention to others\"\n- 3 = \"Interesting: might mention points to others\"\n- 2 = \"Potentially Interesting: doesn't resonate with me\"\n- 1 = \"Not Exciting: doesn't resonate with me\"\n\n## Overall Assessment\n- 5 = \"Consider for Award\" (top 2.5%)\n- 4.5 = \"Borderline Award\"\n- 4 = \"Conference: could be accepted\"\n- 3.5 = \"Borderline Conference\"\n- 3 = \"Findings: could be accepted to Findings\"\n- 2.5 = \"Borderline Findings\"\n- 2 = \"Resubmit next cycle\"\n- 1.5 = \"Resubmit after next cycle\"\n- 1 = \"Do not resubmit\"\n\n## Key Differentiation Guidance\nMain conference papers require stronger novelty and impact; Findings emphasize \"soundness and reproducibility.\"","durationMs":5410,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Cg6NNny9iBjy9XhZwNsvfE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me summarize the key pieces:\n\n**ARR/EMNLP scoring:**\n- Soundness: 1-5\n- Excitement: 1-5\n- Overall Assessment: 1-5 (with 0.5 increments)\n- Confidence: 1-5\n- EMNLP 2025 acceptance rate: ~22%\n\n**Paper summary:**\nTitle: \"Not All Correlations Transfer: When Population-Level Quality Signals Fail at Instance-Level Selection\"\n- Studies correlation-selection gap in TTS for structured IE\n- 3 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), 3 models (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), 5 confidence signals\n\n\n- The core finding is that while these signals show moderate quality estimation performance (correlations up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.75), they completely fail when used for instance selection with improvements under 0.3 percentage points. The authors identify two root causes: instance degeneracy and logprob score compression. They also test a supervised verifier approach which only closes 3.2% of the oracle gap, along with various ablations on LoRA rank, free-form generation, and model scale.\n\nNow I'm setting up a structured review process with two specialized reviewers—one focusing on methodological soundness and experimental rigor, the other on novelty and positioning within the broader literature. I'll have them work independently first, then cross-examine their findings before synthesizing a final assessment.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":620,"cache_read_input_tokens":48307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":620},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"740db59a-54f2-4ccb-ab90-4ae46f110bf2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Cg6NNny9iBjy9XhZwNsvfE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have the full paper, review context, and EMNLP/ARR scoring criteria. Dispatching two parallel reviewer agents (R1: Methods+Experiments, R2: Novelty+Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":620,"cache_read_input_tokens":48307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":620},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"9324580e-7c25-4df2-9bfb-130e039da5b4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Cg6NNny9iBjy9XhZwNsvfE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017WkXRNczfczKngFzyJwyzJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for an EMNLP 2026 Main Conference submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n- Acceptance rate ~22%\n- Scoring scales:\n  - **Soundness**: 1 (Major Issues) – 2 (Poor) – 3 (Acceptable) – 4 (Strong) – 5 (Excellent)\n  - **Excitement**: 1 (Not Exciting) – 2 (Potentially Interesting) – 3 (Interesting) – 4 (Exciting) – 5 (Highly Exciting)\n  - **Overall Assessment**: 1 (Do not resubmit) – 1.5 (Resubmit after next cycle) – 2 (Resubmit next cycle) – 2.5 (Borderline Findings) – 3 (Findings) – 3.5 (Borderline Conference) – 4 (Conference) – 4.5 (Borderline Award) – 5 (Award)\n  - **Confidence**: 1-5\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Not All Correlations Transfer: When Population-Level Quality Signals Fail at Instance-Level Selection\"\n\nThe paper studies test-time scaling (TTS) for structured information extraction (IE). It argues that confidence signals which correlate with output quality at the population level (quality estimation / QE) fail to select better candidates at the instance level. Across 3 datasets (SciERC NER+RE, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), 3 models (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and 5 confidence signal families (Structural Jaccard, Fuzzy-match Kendall, Log-probability, Voting Confidence, Exact Match), they find:\n- Moderate QE: Spearman ρ up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.75\n- Selection failure: Δ < 0.3pp vs greedy, all p > 0.05, despite 13pp oracle headroom\n- Two mechanisms: instance degeneracy (21-58%) and logprob score compression (0.041 nats within-instance range)\n- LoRA rank ablation (rank 8 vs 32): reduces degeneracy 3× but signals still fail\n- Free-form ablation: LP compression intrinsic (std ratio 0.97 constrained vs free)\n- Scale ablation: Qwen3-4B replicates the gap\n- Supervised verifier (LR on 5 signals, 5-fold CV): closes only 3.2% of oracle gap\n- Cross-model: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL replicates (ρ 0.31-0.43, selection ≤0.2pp, degeneracy 56%)\n\nPaper is 6 pages + appendix with numerical consistency notes.\n\n## Key Tables\n- **Table 1**: QE on SciERC. EM AUROC 0.749 (NER), VC ρ 0.379 (NER). Signal ranking task-dependent.\n- **Table 2**: Selection on SciERC. Best signal LP +0.6pp NER (p=0.39, CI [-3.0,+4.2]pp, d=0.038). Oracle +13.2pp NER, +18.1pp RE.\n- **Table 3**: LLaMA CoNLL cross-model. ρ 0.31-0.43, selection ≤0.2pp, degeneracy 56%.\n- **Table 4**: Supervised verifier. LR 5-sig AUROC 0.760 but selection F1 0.648 (+0.4pp), gap closed 3.2%.\n- **Table 5**: Feature importance. High inter-signal correlation (mean ρ 0.45-0.72).\n- **Table 6**: Scale ablation 4B vs 8B. QE preserved, selection fails at both scales.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (full planned experiments, paper may not cover all)\nThe experiment plan included:\n1. **ner_llama_conll** (exp-017): LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL. This IS in the paper (Table 3). Note: both models are 8B decoder-only with identical LoRA recipe → a reviewer might argue this isn't true \"model-agnostic\" validation. Needs encoder-based or 70B+ model.\n2. **simple_verifier_baseline** (exp-019): LR verifier on 5 signals. This IS in the paper (Table 4). Note: LR on same 5 correlated signals is essentially a weighted average. A real verifier would use input representations. Gap closure 3.2% actually underperforms best single signal EM (4.6%).\n3. **claim_audit** (exp-024): Found numerical inconsistencies. Abstract originally claimed \"AUROC up to 0.82\" — the paper now claims 0.75, but check carefully. Greedy F1 had 3 different values across versions; paper says 0.644. R²=0.94 claim on n=3 was downgraded to \"qualitative\" but may still appear.\n\n## Your Review Focus (R1: Methods + Experiments)\nEvaluate in depth:\n1. **Experimental design soundness**: Are the experiments well-designed to support the claims? Are controls appropriate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap tests, confidence intervals, effect sizes — are they sufficient? Is p=0.39 appropriately interpreted?\n3. **Ablation quality**: Do the LoRA rank, free-form, and scale ablations properly isolate variables?\n4. **Numerical consistency**: Check all numbers across abstract, intro, tables, discussion. Flag any contradictions.\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient? Models, hyperparameters, seeds, data splits.\n6. **Coverage**: What experiments are missing that would strengthen the claims? (encoder models, 70B+, larger N, more datasets, better verifier)\n7. **Cross-model validation strength**: Two 8B decoder-only models with same LoRA setup — is this really model-agnostic?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with references to tables/sections)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Missing Experiments / Suggestions**\n6. **Numerical Consistency Check** (list any discrepancies found)\n7. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 scale with 0.5 increments), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a top venue with 22% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":620,"cache_read_input_tokens":48307,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":620},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"420e0bd3-109a-40b5-8857-f24de7585ee0"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a3cded80d45c60cc1","tool_use_id":"toolu_017WkXRNczfczKngFzyJwyzJ","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for an EMNLP 2026 Main Conference submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n- Acceptance rate ~22%\n- Scoring scales:\n  - **Soundness**: 1 (Major Issues) – 2 (Poor) – 3 (Acceptable) – 4 (Strong) – 5 (Excellent)\n  - **Excitement**: 1 (Not Exciting) – 2 (Potentially Interesting) – 3 (Interesting) – 4 (Exciting) – 5 (Highly Exciting)\n  - **Overall Assessment**: 1 (Do not resubmit) – 1.5 (Resubmit after next cycle) – 2 (Resubmit next cycle) – 2.5 (Borderline Findings) – 3 (Findings) – 3.5 (Borderline Conference) – 4 (Conference) – 4.5 (Borderline Award) – 5 (Award)\n  - **Confidence**: 1-5\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Not All Correlations Transfer: When Population-Level Quality Signals Fail at Instance-Level Selection\"\n\nThe paper studies test-time scaling (TTS) for structured information extraction (IE). It argues that confidence signals which correlate with output quality at the population level (quality estimation / QE) fail to select better candidates at the instance level. Across 3 datasets (SciERC NER+RE, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), 3 models (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and 5 confidence signal families (Structural Jaccard, Fuzzy-match Kendall, Log-probability, Voting Confidence, Exact Match), they find:\n- Moderate QE: Spearman ρ up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.75\n- Selection failure: Δ < 0.3pp vs greedy, all p > 0.05, despite 13pp oracle headroom\n- Two mechanisms: instance degeneracy (21-58%) and logprob score compression (0.041 nats within-instance range)\n- LoRA rank ablation (rank 8 vs 32): reduces degeneracy 3× but signals still fail\n- Free-form ablation: LP compression intrinsic (std ratio 0.97 constrained vs free)\n- Scale ablation: Qwen3-4B replicates the gap\n- Supervised verifier (LR on 5 signals, 5-fold CV): closes only 3.2% of oracle gap\n- Cross-model: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL replicates (ρ 0.31-0.43, selection ≤0.2pp, degeneracy 56%)\n\nPaper is 6 pages + appendix with numerical consistency notes.\n\n## Key Tables\n- **Table 1**: QE on SciERC. EM AUROC 0.749 (NER), VC ρ 0.379 (NER). Signal ranking task-dependent.\n- **Table 2**: Selection on SciERC. Best signal LP +0.6pp NER (p=0.39, CI [-3.0,+4.2]pp, d=0.038). Oracle +13.2pp NER, +18.1pp RE.\n- **Table 3**: LLaMA CoNLL cross-model. ρ 0.31-0.43, selection ≤0.2pp, degeneracy 56%.\n- **Table 4**: Supervised verifier. LR 5-sig AUROC 0.760 but selection F1 0.648 (+0.4pp), gap closed 3.2%.\n- **Table 5**: Feature importance. High inter-signal correlation (mean ρ 0.45-0.72).\n- **Table 6**: Scale ablation 4B vs 8B. QE preserved, selection fails at both scales.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (full planned experiments, paper may not cover all)\nThe experiment plan included:\n1. **ner_llama_conll** (exp-017): LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL. This IS in the paper (Table 3). Note: both models are 8B decoder-only with identical LoRA recipe → a reviewer might argue this isn't true \"model-agnostic\" validation. Needs encoder-based or 70B+ model.\n2. **simple_verifier_baseline** (exp-019): LR verifier on 5 signals. This IS in the paper (Table 4). Note: LR on same 5 correlated signals is essentially a weighted average. A real verifier would use input representations. Gap closure 3.2% actually underperforms best single signal EM (4.6%).\n3. **claim_audit** (exp-024): Found numerical inconsistencies. Abstract originally claimed \"AUROC up to 0.82\" — the paper now claims 0.75, but check carefully. Greedy F1 had 3 different values across versions; paper says 0.644. R²=0.94 claim on n=3 was downgraded to \"qualitative\" but may still appear.\n\n## Your Review Focus (R1: Methods + Experiments)\nEvaluate in depth:\n1. **Experimental design soundness**: Are the experiments well-designed to support the claims? Are controls appropriate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap tests, confidence intervals, effect sizes — are they sufficient? Is p=0.39 appropriately interpreted?\n3. **Ablation quality**: Do the LoRA rank, free-form, and scale ablations properly isolate variables?\n4. **Numerical consistency**: Check all numbers across abstract, intro, tables, discussion. Flag any contradictions.\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient? Models, hyperparameters, seeds, data splits.\n6. **Coverage**: What experiments are missing that would strengthen the claims? (encoder models, 70B+, larger N, more datasets, better verifier)\n7. **Cross-model validation strength**: Two 8B decoder-only models with same LoRA setup — is this really model-agnostic?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with references to tables/sections)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Missing Experiments / Suggestions**\n6. **Numerical Consistency Check** (list any discrepancies found)\n7. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 scale with 0.5 increments), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a top venue with 22% acceptance rate.","uuid":"188b260d-039a-41c6-8c21-b64c6c7adf0c","session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** for an EMNLP 2026 Main Conference submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ACL Rolling Review)\n- Acceptance rate ~22%\n- Scoring scales:\n  - **Soundness**: 1 (Major Issues) – 2 (Poor) – 3 (Acceptable) – 4 (Strong) – 5 (Excellent)\n  - **Excitement**: 1 (Not Exciting) – 2 (Potentially Interesting) – 3 (Interesting) – 4 (Exciting) – 5 (Highly Exciting)\n  - **Overall Assessment**: 1 (Do not resubmit) – 1.5 (Resubmit after next cycle) – 2 (Resubmit next cycle) – 2.5 (Borderline Findings) – 3 (Findings) – 3.5 (Borderline Conference) – 4 (Conference) – 4.5 (Borderline Award) – 5 (Award)\n  - **Confidence**: 1-5\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Not All Correlations Transfer: When Population-Level Quality Signals Fail at Instance-Level Selection\"\n\nThe paper studies test-time scaling (TTS) for structured information extraction (IE). It argues that confidence signals which correlate with output quality at the population level (quality estimation / QE) fail to select better candidates at the instance level. Across 3 datasets (SciERC NER+RE, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), 3 models (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and 5 confidence signal families (Structural Jaccard, Fuzzy-match Kendall, Log-probability, Voting Confidence, Exact Match), they find:\n- Moderate QE: Spearman ρ up to 0.45, AUROC up to 0.75\n- Selection failure: Δ < 0.3pp vs greedy, all p > 0.05, despite 13pp oracle headroom\n- Two mechanisms: instance degeneracy (21-58%) and logprob score compression (0.041 nats within-instance range)\n- LoRA rank ablation (rank 8 vs 32): reduces degeneracy 3× but signals still fail\n- Free-form ablation: LP compression intrinsic (std ratio 0.97 constrained vs free)\n- Scale ablation: Qwen3-4B replicates the gap\n- Supervised verifier (LR on 5 signals, 5-fold CV): closes only 3.2% of oracle gap\n- Cross-model: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL replicates (ρ 0.31-0.43, selection ≤0.2pp, degeneracy 56%)\n\nPaper is 6 pages + appendix with numerical consistency notes.\n\n## Key Tables\n- **Table 1**: QE on SciERC. EM AUROC 0.749 (NER), VC ρ 0.379 (NER). Signal ranking task-dependent.\n- **Table 2**: Selection on SciERC. Best signal LP +0.6pp NER (p=0.39, CI [-3.0,+4.2]pp, d=0.038). Oracle +13.2pp NER, +18.1pp RE.\n- **Table 3**: LLaMA CoNLL cross-model. ρ 0.31-0.43, selection ≤0.2pp, degeneracy 56%.\n- **Table 4**: Supervised verifier. LR 5-sig AUROC 0.760 but selection F1 0.648 (+0.4pp), gap closed 3.2%.\n- **Table 5**: Feature importance. High inter-signal correlation (mean ρ 0.45-0.72).\n- **Table 6**: Scale ablation 4B vs 8B. QE preserved, selection fails at both scales.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (full planned experiments, paper may not cover all)\nThe experiment plan included:\n1. **ner_llama_conll** (exp-017): LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL. This IS in the paper (Table 3). Note: both models are 8B decoder-only with identical LoRA recipe → a reviewer might argue this isn't true \"model-agnostic\" validation. Needs encoder-based or 70B+ model.\n2. **simple_verifier_baseline** (exp-019): LR verifier on 5 signals. This IS in the paper (Table 4). Note: LR on same 5 correlated signals is essentially a weighted average. A real verifier would use input representations. Gap closure 3.2% actually underperforms best single signal EM (4.6%).\n3. **claim_audit** (exp-024): Found numerical inconsistencies. Abstract originally claimed \"AUROC up to 0.82\" — the paper now claims 0.75, but check carefully. Greedy F1 had 3 different values across versions; paper says 0.644. R²=0.94 claim on n=3 was downgraded to \"qualitative\" but may still appear.\n\n## Your Review Focus (R1: Methods + Experiments)\nEvaluate in depth:\n1. **Experimental design soundness**: Are the experiments well-designed to support the claims? Are controls appropriate?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Bootstrap tests, confidence intervals, effect sizes — are they sufficient? Is p=0.39 appropriately interpreted?\n3. **Ablation quality**: Do the LoRA rank, free-form, and scale ablations properly isolate variables?\n4. **Numerical consistency**: Check all numbers across abstract, intro, tables, discussion. Flag any contradictions.\n5. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient? Models, hyperparameters, seeds, data splits.\n6. **Coverage**: What experiments are missing that would strengthen the claims? (encoder models, 70B+, larger N, more datasets, better verifier)\n7. **Cross-model validation strength**: Two 8B decoder-only models with same LoRA setup — is this really model-agnostic?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce a structured review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with references to tables/sections)\n4. **Questions for Authors**\n5. **Missing Experiments / Suggestions**\n6. **Numerical Consistency Check** (list any discrepancies found)\n7. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5 scale with 0.5 increments), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. This is a top venue with 22% acceptance rate."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017WkXRNczfczKngFzyJwyzJ","session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"c10099a8-42cd-4412-88f6-e80cbd4e9374","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:13:10.067Z"}
