{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H1xwqpjo9ab5CoaRD9bGHe","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R1 Reaction to R2's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nR2's novelty critique is well-taken and strengthens my overall assessment. The observation that instance degeneracy on CoNLL is \"partially tautological\" aligns directly with my concern (W7) about conflating identical outputs with same-F1 outputs. I agree the core negative finding — capable models produce concentrated outputs leaving no room for selection — is less surprising than the paper frames it. R2's identification of missing related work on diverse decoding and MBR diminishing returns is a genuine gap I underweighted.\n\nI strongly agree on the statistical power concern (W7 in R2, my W5). Two seeds with n=529 is insufficient to confidently claim a negative result. R2 and I independently flagged the weak supervised verifier (my W3, R2's W6), which reinforces that this is a real methodological gap rather than a nitpick.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nR2 rates Soundness at 4.0 versus my 3.5. I maintain the lower score is justified. The AUROC 0.82 claim inconsistency (my W1) and the p-value discrepancy between 0.29 and 0.39 (my W4) are concrete numerical errors that R2 does not address. These are not framing issues — they are factual reliability problems that should pull soundness down.\n\nI also disagree slightly with R2's characterization that the instance degeneracy finding is purely \"definitional.\" The paper does provide empirical measurement of concentration rates across models and temperatures, which has some informational value even if the qualitative direction is unsurprising. The issue is magnitude estimation, not zero novelty.\n\n**New Points from R2**\n\nR2's observation about the non-tied LP subset (+2.9pp, p=0.004) being a genuine positive selection result is sharp and something I missed. This reframes the paper's narrative: there is evidence that selection works precisely when degeneracy breaks, which actually supports a more nuanced and interesting conclusion than the blanket negative. The authors burying this is a missed opportunity.\n\nSimilarly, R2's point about the RE free-form ablation (+1.8pp) being relegated to the appendix is well-observed. These positive sub-results deserve prominence and would make the paper more useful.\n\nThe missing comparison with Yao et al. (2026) is a legitimate gap I did not flag.\n\n**Score Adjustments**\n\nAfter reading R2, I would revise slightly downward:\n\n- **Excitement**: 3 → 2.5. R2 convinced me the novelty is lower than I initially credited. The core findings decompose into known phenomena, and the paper's main contribution is empirical confirmation rather than new insight.\n- **Soundness**: 3.5 → 3.5 (unchanged). R2's higher score of 4 is not warranted given the numerical inconsistencies I identified.\n- **Overall**: 3.0 → 2.75. The combination of limited novelty (per R2) and methodological issues (per my review) makes this borderline. The QE vs. selection framework and the positive sub-results (non-tied LP, RE free-form) are genuinely useful but underexploited by the authors themselves.\n\n**Summary**: R2's novelty critique is the strongest argument against this paper and complements my methods-focused concerns. Together, our reviews converge on the same overall score but for complementary reasons — limited novelty plus insufficient experimental rigor for the claims made."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: acc27a8acf4c8c1a4 (use SendMessage with to: 'acc27a8acf4c8c1a4' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15721\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 21351</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"7a773d2b-887c-4346-8c77-c5a1a097a4d3","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:16:56.707Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** in a roundtable discussion. You focus on **Methods and Experiments**. You have already completed your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 2's review (focused on Novelty + Related Work) and providing your reactions.\n\n## Your R1 Independent Review Summary\nYour scores: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3, Overall 3.0 (Findings).\nKey concerns: (1) AUROC 0.82 claim inconsistency, (2) narrow cross-model validation (all decoder-only 3-8B with same LoRA), (3) weak supervised verifier baseline (LR on correlated features), (4) p-value inconsistency (0.29 vs 0.39), (5) only 2-seed mean for selection, (6) temperature only T=0.7 and T=1.0 tested, (7) instance degeneracy definition conflates identical outputs vs same-F1 outputs.\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\nR2 scores: Soundness 4, Excitement 2, Overall 3.0 (Findings).\n\nR2's key points:\n**Strengths identified**: Clear QE vs selection distinction, thorough experimental methodology, honest negative result with mechanistic depth, practical value of QE framework, well-written.\n\n**Weaknesses identified**:\n1. Limited novelty — \"capable models produce similar outputs, leaving little room for selection\" is a straightforward consequence of model convergence already observed (Kirk et al. diversity collapse, MBR diminishing returns, Brown et al. Large Language Monkeys).\n2. Narrow task scope (NER + exploratory RE) undermines generality claims in the title/framing.\n3. Instance degeneracy for CoNLL is \"partially tautological\" — near-perfect model produces same output every time.\n4. 3-8B model scale only — 70B+ might differ.\n5. Missing direct quantitative comparison with Yao et al. (2026), the most comparable prior work.\n6. Weak supervised verifier (same as your W3).\n7. Statistical power concerns for primary negative result (2-seed, n=529).\n\n**Novelty assessment**: Medium-Low. Core findings decompose into: (a) instance-level signals cannot by construction select among candidates (definitional), (b) capable models produce concentrated outputs (known), (c) LP is weak for structured extraction (expected).\n\n**Missing related work**: Diverse decoding (Vijayakumar et al., Ippolito et al.), broader MBR literature, structured prediction calibration, ensemble selection in NLP, output diversity/mode collapse in fine-tuning, token-level vs sequence-level scoring.\n\n**Interesting point**: R2 notes the non-tied LP subset (+2.9pp, p=0.004) as actually a positive selection result, and asks why the RE free-form ablation (+1.8pp) is buried in the appendix.\n\n## Your Task\nReact to R2's review:\n1. Which of R2's points do you **agree** with and would strengthen your review?\n2. Which of R2's points do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Does R2's novelty assessment change your view of the paper's overall merit?\n4. Are there any new concerns or strengths R2 raised that you missed?\n5. Would you adjust any of your scores after reading R2's review? If so, how?\n\nKeep your response under 500 words. Be specific.","agentId":"acc27a8acf4c8c1a4","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R1 Reaction to R2's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nR2's novelty critique is well-taken and strengthens my overall assessment. The observation that instance degeneracy on CoNLL is \"partially tautological\" aligns directly with my concern (W7) about conflating identical outputs with same-F1 outputs. I agree the core negative finding — capable models produce concentrated outputs leaving no room for selection — is less surprising than the paper frames it. R2's identification of missing related work on diverse decoding and MBR diminishing returns is a genuine gap I underweighted.\n\nI strongly agree on the statistical power concern (W7 in R2, my W5). Two seeds with n=529 is insufficient to confidently claim a negative result. R2 and I independently flagged the weak supervised verifier (my W3, R2's W6), which reinforces that this is a real methodological gap rather than a nitpick.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nR2 rates Soundness at 4.0 versus my 3.5. I maintain the lower score is justified. The AUROC 0.82 claim inconsistency (my W1) and the p-value discrepancy between 0.29 and 0.39 (my W4) are concrete numerical errors that R2 does not address. These are not framing issues — they are factual reliability problems that should pull soundness down.\n\nI also disagree slightly with R2's characterization that the instance degeneracy finding is purely \"definitional.\" The paper does provide empirical measurement of concentration rates across models and temperatures, which has some informational value even if the qualitative direction is unsurprising. The issue is magnitude estimation, not zero novelty.\n\n**New Points from R2**\n\nR2's observation about the non-tied LP subset (+2.9pp, p=0.004) being a genuine positive selection result is sharp and something I missed. This reframes the paper's narrative: there is evidence that selection works precisely when degeneracy breaks, which actually supports a more nuanced and interesting conclusion than the blanket negative. The authors burying this is a missed opportunity.\n\nSimilarly, R2's point about the RE free-form ablation (+1.8pp) being relegated to the appendix is well-observed. These positive sub-results deserve prominence and would make the paper more useful.\n\nThe missing comparison with Yao et al. (2026) is a legitimate gap I did not flag.\n\n**Score Adjustments**\n\nAfter reading R2, I would revise slightly downward:\n\n- **Excitement**: 3 → 2.5. R2 convinced me the novelty is lower than I initially credited. The core findings decompose into known phenomena, and the paper's main contribution is empirical confirmation rather than new insight.\n- **Soundness**: 3.5 → 3.5 (unchanged). R2's higher score of 4 is not warranted given the numerical inconsistencies I identified.\n- **Overall**: 3.0 → 2.75. The combination of limited novelty (per R2) and methodological issues (per my review) makes this borderline. The QE vs. selection framework and the positive sub-results (non-tied LP, RE free-form) are genuinely useful but underexploited by the authors themselves.\n\n**Summary**: R2's novelty critique is the strongest argument against this paper and complements my methods-focused concerns. Together, our reviews converge on the same overall score but for complementary reasons — limited novelty plus insufficient experimental rigor for the claims made."}],"totalDurationMs":21351,"totalTokens":15721,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6655,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"output_tokens":812,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6655},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":812,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6655,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6655,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"af2ab5b7b6f7367e0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VVk7ye5BVBUHf14PdSfkRX","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2 reacts to R1's review","usage":{"total_tokens":14969,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":22947},"uuid":"7e8be8c6-5cd4-4ad3-9f7e-65c655897fd6","session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VVk7ye5BVBUHf14PdSfkRX","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Reaction to R1's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nR1's weakness #2 (narrow cross-model validation) directly reinforces my concern #2 (narrow task scope) and #4 (3-8B scale only). We converge from different angles on the same conclusion: the paper's generality claims are unsupported. R1's framing — \"two 8B models with identical LoRA recipe doesn't prove model-agnostic\" — is sharper than mine and I'd adopt it.\n\nR1's weakness #3 (weak supervised verifier) matches my concern #6 exactly. R1's technical decomposition — 4/5 features identical across candidates making it effectively univariate — is a stronger articulation than what I had. This matters because the paper uses verifier underperformance to argue unsupervised metrics suffice, but the comparison is rigged.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nR1 gives \"statistical rigor\" as a strength. I partially disagree. Yes, the paper uses many statistical tools (bootstrap, TOST, Cohen's d, Bonferroni), but R1 themselves then identify p-value inconsistencies (0.29 vs 0.39, different tests, different seed counts) and the 2-seed selection problem. Deploying many statistical tests while being inconsistent in their application is not rigor — it's methodological clutter that creates a false impression of thoroughness. I would not list this as a strength.\n\nR1 rates Excitement 3 vs my 2. I think R1's own findings support the lower score. The AUROC claim inflation (0.82 headline from CoNLL, not the primary testbed), numerical version-drift throughout, and the effectively univariate verifier all point to a paper that oversells modest findings. The core observation — that self-consistency agreement correlates with correctness — remains a straightforward consequence of model convergence, as I argued.\n\n**Impact of Numerical Consistency Analysis**\n\nR1's detailed cross-check is the most valuable new contribution. I had flagged statistical power concerns but did not perform table-by-table verification. The supervised verifier discrepancy (0.8% gap / AUROC 0.697 in main text vs 3.2% gap / AUROC 0.760 in provenance) is particularly damaging — these are not rounding differences but materially different results that suggest the paper was assembled from inconsistent experimental runs. The oracle NER discrepancy (0.778 vs 0.776) is minor individually but compounds the pattern. This lowers my confidence in soundness.\n\n**New Concerns I Missed**\n\nThe AUROC 0.82 headline claim sourced from CoNLL rather than SciERC is a presentation integrity issue I should have caught. The WNUT gold-empty filtering (46.5% removal) is also significant — removing nearly half the evaluation set without adequate justification weakens that entire experimental arm.\n\n**Score Adjustments**\n\nAfter reading R1, I would adjust:\n- **Soundness**: 4 → 3.5 (numerical inconsistencies across tables/provenance indicate insufficient quality control)\n- **Excitement**: remains 2 (R1's findings reinforce rather than counter my novelty concerns)\n- **Overall**: 3.0 → 2.75\n\nThe paper has a reasonable core idea but the execution and presentation issues, now documented by both reviewers, place it below the acceptance threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af2ab5b7b6f7367e0 (use SendMessage with to: 'af2ab5b7b6f7367e0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15758\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 22948</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"6de797c0-ac33-4592-9570-045e310de82c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T12:17:14.701Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** in a roundtable discussion. You focus on **Novelty and Related Work**. You have already completed your independent review. Now you are reading Reviewer 1's review (focused on Methods + Experiments) and providing your reactions.\n\n## Your R2 Independent Review Summary\nYour scores: Soundness 4, Excitement 2, Overall 3.0 (Findings).\nKey concerns: (1) Limited novelty — core observation is straightforward consequence of model convergence, (2) narrow task scope undermines generality claims, (3) CoNLL degeneracy is partially tautological, (4) 3-8B scale only, (5) missing comparison with Yao et al., (6) weak supervised verifier, (7) statistical power concerns. Novelty: Medium-Low.\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\nR1 scores: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3, Overall 3.0 (Findings).\n\nR1's key points:\n**Strengths**: Well-defined conceptual contribution, thorough ablation chain, statistical rigor (bootstrap, TOST, Cohen's d, Bonferroni), difficulty gradient design (SciERC/CoNLL/WNUT-17 with explicit roles), extensive appendix, honest limitations.\n\n**Major Weaknesses**:\n1. **AUROC 0.82 claim inconsistency**: Abstract/intro claim \"AUROC up to 0.82\" but this comes from CoNLL EM (0.816 rounded up), not the primary test bed SciERC (0.749). The paper_provenance documents this was supposed to be corrected to 0.75 but wasn't.\n2. **Narrow cross-model validation**: All decoder-only, all LoRA, all 3-8B. Two 8B models with identical LoRA recipe doesn't prove \"model-agnostic.\"\n3. **Weak supervised verifier**: LR on 5 correlated features where 4/5 are instance-level (identical across candidates) → effectively univariate LP selector. Not a fair test of learned scoring.\n4. **P-value inconsistency**: Table 2 shows p=0.29 (2-seed sign test) vs provenance p=0.39 (3-seed bootstrap). Different tests on different seed counts without reconciliation.\n5. **Oracle NER value**: Table 2 says 0.778, provenance says 0.776.\n\n**Minor Weaknesses**: WNUT gold-empty filtering (46.5% removed), temperature sensitivity under-explored, 2-seed selection means, free-form ablation limited to one model, degeneracy definition conflates identical outputs vs same-F1.\n\n**Numerical consistency check**: R1 did a detailed table cross-checking abstract/intro/tables/discussion values. Found several version-drift issues — the supervised verifier numbers differ between main text (0.8% gap, AUROC 0.697) and provenance (3.2% gap, AUROC 0.760).\n\n**Missing experiments**: Encoder-based model, higher temperature ablation, 70B+, stronger verifier (MLP on hidden states), more RE benchmarks, nucleus sampling.\n\n## Your Task\nReact to R1's review:\n1. Which of R1's points do you **agree** with and would strengthen your review?\n2. Which of R1's points do you **disagree** with or see differently?\n3. Does R1's numerical consistency analysis change your view of soundness?\n4. Are there any new concerns or strengths R1 raised that you missed?\n5. Would you adjust any of your scores after reading R1's review? If so, how?\n\nKeep your response under 500 words. Be specific.","agentId":"af2ab5b7b6f7367e0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Reaction to R1's Review\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nR1's weakness #2 (narrow cross-model validation) directly reinforces my concern #2 (narrow task scope) and #4 (3-8B scale only). We converge from different angles on the same conclusion: the paper's generality claims are unsupported. R1's framing — \"two 8B models with identical LoRA recipe doesn't prove model-agnostic\" — is sharper than mine and I'd adopt it.\n\nR1's weakness #3 (weak supervised verifier) matches my concern #6 exactly. R1's technical decomposition — 4/5 features identical across candidates making it effectively univariate — is a stronger articulation than what I had. This matters because the paper uses verifier underperformance to argue unsupervised metrics suffice, but the comparison is rigged.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nR1 gives \"statistical rigor\" as a strength. I partially disagree. Yes, the paper uses many statistical tools (bootstrap, TOST, Cohen's d, Bonferroni), but R1 themselves then identify p-value inconsistencies (0.29 vs 0.39, different tests, different seed counts) and the 2-seed selection problem. Deploying many statistical tests while being inconsistent in their application is not rigor — it's methodological clutter that creates a false impression of thoroughness. I would not list this as a strength.\n\nR1 rates Excitement 3 vs my 2. I think R1's own findings support the lower score. The AUROC claim inflation (0.82 headline from CoNLL, not the primary testbed), numerical version-drift throughout, and the effectively univariate verifier all point to a paper that oversells modest findings. The core observation — that self-consistency agreement correlates with correctness — remains a straightforward consequence of model convergence, as I argued.\n\n**Impact of Numerical Consistency Analysis**\n\nR1's detailed cross-check is the most valuable new contribution. I had flagged statistical power concerns but did not perform table-by-table verification. The supervised verifier discrepancy (0.8% gap / AUROC 0.697 in main text vs 3.2% gap / AUROC 0.760 in provenance) is particularly damaging — these are not rounding differences but materially different results that suggest the paper was assembled from inconsistent experimental runs. The oracle NER discrepancy (0.778 vs 0.776) is minor individually but compounds the pattern. This lowers my confidence in soundness.\n\n**New Concerns I Missed**\n\nThe AUROC 0.82 headline claim sourced from CoNLL rather than SciERC is a presentation integrity issue I should have caught. The WNUT gold-empty filtering (46.5% removal) is also significant — removing nearly half the evaluation set without adequate justification weakens that entire experimental arm.\n\n**Score Adjustments**\n\nAfter reading R1, I would adjust:\n- **Soundness**: 4 → 3.5 (numerical inconsistencies across tables/provenance indicate insufficient quality control)\n- **Excitement**: remains 2 (R1's findings reinforce rather than counter my novelty concerns)\n- **Overall**: 3.0 → 2.75\n\nThe paper has a reasonable core idea but the execution and presentation issues, now documented by both reviewers, place it below the acceptance threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":22948,"totalTokens":15758,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6713,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"output_tokens":791,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6713},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":791,"cache_read_input_tokens":8251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6713,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015GxBBhmMThNZqaUjd13HDm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Step 2 (cross-sharing) is done. Both reviewers have reacted to each other's reviews and adjusted scores. Now I need to do Step 3 (convergence) and Step 4 (synthesis/final report).\n\nLet me summarize the convergence:\n\n**Post-roundtable convergence:**\n- R1 adjusted: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2.5, Overall 2.75\n- R2 adjusted: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 2, Overall 2.75\n\n**Areas of agreement:**\n1. Narrow model diversity (all decoder-only 3-8B LoRA) — generality claims unsupported\n\n\n2. Weak supervised verifier (effectively univariate)\n3. Numerical inconsistencies undermine credibility\n4. Core observation has limited novelty\n5. The paper has practical value (QE framework) but not enough for main conference\n6. Both converge on ~2.75 overall (Borderline Findings)\n7. Non-tied LP subset result (+2.9pp) is genuinely interesting but underexploited\n8. AUROC 0.82 headline claim is misleading\n\n**Areas of divergence:**\n- R1 initially gave higher excitement (3→2.5) vs R2 (2, unchanged)\n- R1 listed statistical rigor as a strength, though both ultimately agreed on the same final score\n\nNow I'm pulling together the consolidated review report and preparing to submit it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3886,"cache_read_input_tokens":60900,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3886},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b72398d7-e431-4137-b17e-51fc8746e976","uuid":"8f22bc00-5d73-4d8e-8f4c-c4d56437601a"}
